WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:06.254
What you are hearing is a synthetic clone of my voice, generated locally with my consent.

00:00:06.304 --> 00:00:07.792
I am Dominic Marrocco.

00:00:07.842 --> 00:00:20.100
This is MOMM, the Mixture of Model Modality: every frontier model on your machine, reviewing your work together, while the agent you are talking to stays the only one allowed to write.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.190
Here is the problem it solves.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.861
Today's coding agents grade their own homework.

00:00:25.911 --> 00:00:32.349
The model that writes a change also reviews it, with the same blind spots on both sides.

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:35.020
When the session ends, the reasoning evaporates.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:39.600
And a second opinion usually means another API key to pay for.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:45.655
It turns the models you already subscribe to into a standing review coalition.

00:00:45.705 --> 00:00:49.697
Your agent, the governor, pipes a diff to the dispatcher.

00:00:49.747 --> 00:00:53.739
A readiness check probes every route with zero model calls.

00:00:53.789 --> 00:01:01.420
Then the other installed reviewers, from four different companies, review it in parallel, each through its own official login.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:09.100
Every API key variable is stripped, and a route without a login is refused before any tokens are spent.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:11.660
Reviewers are read-only instruments.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.300
Their findings are claims, not verdicts.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:22.220
Before a single line changes, the governor must reproduce each critical or warning finding with a failing test.

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:28.820
Every suggestion gets an explicit disposition, applied or rejected with a reason, on the record.

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:31.900
The governor never reviews its own work.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:34.284
Every run is sealed.

00:01:34.334 --> 00:01:39.432
The report is content-addressed and bound to the exact input by its hash.

00:01:39.482 --> 00:01:44.184
Your private ledger lives beside your project and never leaves your machine.

00:01:44.234 --> 00:01:52.500
This project reviews itself with MOMM and publishes its sanitised evidence, so every number on the page resolves to a hash.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.732
And it is not only code.

00:01:55.782 --> 00:02:10.730
Hand MOMM a manuscript, a PDF, or an image, and the same protocol becomes editorial peer review: parallel referees with different training, an editor who must reproduce before deciding, and a decision ledger that survives the session.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:16.000
Put your reviewer guidelines in a rules file, and every referee follows them.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:20.262
One command installs it into every harness you have.

00:02:20.312 --> 00:02:24.877
Then just tell your agent: use momm to review my current changes.

00:02:24.927 --> 00:02:25.262
Relax.

00:02:25.312 --> 00:02:26.800
It is already over.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:32.500
[Synthetic voice: a consented local clone of Dominic Marrocco's voice, F5-TTS via Promptus]
